./LOGBOOK.md:930:- DECISION: M0 uses fixed schema constants and unilateral local caps; nothing new on the wire beyond the existing `maxFrame` min-negotiation. Hello flag bit 1, feature bit 1, and message types `0x40–0x4F` reserved (unallocated) for an evidence-gated M3 limits exchange. Rationale: peer-advertised limits are unverifiable claims (local hard caps stay mandatory regardless), the datagram ceiling is HEV-pinned so negotiation has nothing to negotiate, and manifest-verified relay upload version-locks peer pairs. Full record: board outcome `TASK-260715-18owh7_decision.md`; awaiting review acceptance (proposed ADR-021).
./LOGBOOK.md:931:- FINDING: Pinned HEV caps UDP-in-TCP copy buffers at `UDP_BUF_SIZE = 1500` (`hev-socks5-udp.c:26`); `recvmmsg_tcp` returns `-1` (stream-fatal, not a drop) when `datlen > 1500 − addrlen` (`hev-socks5-udp.c:203-206`). With ADR-020 keeping HEV unmodified, the relay→client reply path physically cannot carry a UDP payload above 1481 bytes (IPv6). This, plus unfragmented 1500-MTU egress, fixes `maxUDPPayload = 1472` as a v1 wire constant; raising it is an HEV-fork decision, not a protocol negotiation.
