      "verifiedIndependently": "Both findings were re-checked before being accepted. F1: the claim that withholding costs the host nothing was verified against Apple's own text rather than assumed. 'Sharing access to keychain items among a collection of apps' states that the application identifier entitlement is added to every app at signing, that the system recognizes it as the app's default keychain access group, that items stored in it are private to the app, and that 'if you don't specify any keychain access groups, then the app ID is the default'. TN3137 states that macOS builds the access-group list from code signing entitlements authorized by a provisioning profile, and macos.host is provisioned on both channels, so the default group is available. The same source also yields the one genuine consequence the reviewer did not name: the default group MOVES when the entitlement is withdrawn, which is now recorded as a downstream requirement rather than discovered at first build. F3: TASK-260715-1jckn0's scope and acceptance criteria were read in full; they name NETunnelProviderManager save/load/enable/start/status/app-message/stop, one versioned provider response, and clean stop, and no shared container, UserDefaults suite, or snapshot anywhere. The r4 rationale's claim that the probe exercises the production shared-container path is not supported by that contract. BOTH CONFIRMED.",
